#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100961 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "506f52f3-8093-5bee-a2f5-f30f064dc715" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With One Area"}] :question/id #uuid "caa282bb-e637-522c-a2da-b384b1ff855a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10012" :question/problem "![](images/3e/3e9a879522107eb2b2381a688d1173fdf6a93a8b81686fd2f21c815008ec35df.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of $f'(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $10.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=3.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and $f'(x)\\neq 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,\\,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =3 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3+(-10) \\\\  & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{3,-7\\} = 3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100698 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "be6b45d4-9257-5a84-8e19-50a315871d3e" :question/math-academy-id "e-442" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $1 \\leq x \\leq 5.$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below. Find a value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-2.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4327bc76ae23907fb628cc9e9d5d045ab773070f9ce8fc2972dcf78e434b24d.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=2$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,2,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/7f/7f129ef5fe5d219f1bf57425682cea13ff657b9a539dc7ae0b2d172bf37c2c28.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ f(4) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}-1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{3}{2}-1+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} =  \\max\\left\\lbrace -2,-\\dfrac{1}{2},-\\dfrac{3}{2},-1 \\right\\rbrace = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=-\\dfrac{1}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100699 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5bcc62c-f716-503f-9195-b1f8f1c78e04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/id #uuid "9fbd4cac-e923-57fd-94f3-79e323195d1b" :question/math-academy-id "e-443" :question/problem "The function $f$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,6].$ The graph of $f'(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown below, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,6]$ given that $f(0)=1.$\n\n![](images/7c/7ca8e07d3438890b4f1e220e491d4a20f35c7473a31e76426b9a1fa13ed6926c.png)" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that $f'(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,6),$ and $f'(x)=0$ at $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,4,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ f(6) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6-2 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\nf_{\\text{max}} = \\max\\{1, 7, 5\\} = 7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is $f_{\\text{max}}=7,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is $x_\\text{max}=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100961 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "506f52f3-8093-5bee-a2f5-f30f064dc715" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cdd0cee-d5dc-55dd-b18b-edd5c5d143b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098296} {:answer/id #uuid "a95de19e-8a0c-5ea7-a40e-8653db2879e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098297} {:answer/id #uuid "c73cb9d0-0bfe-5fae-8bc1-10f38468113d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098298} {:answer/id #uuid "db6c4cce-5a1e-5047-98c5-fd057127d3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098299} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8cc2f3-9815-5b94-8ea4-2375914bf19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f8cc2f3-9815-5b94-8ea4-2375914bf19e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098300} :answer-field/id #uuid "aaaebb3c-11a8-5d61-b73a-97d31a924d49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186098295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b935d32-f672-54cd-918e-5cf82b270c6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141616" :question/problem "![](images/06/0669cf5e31d559c70c22c5154c0e43638eb5df8a11bedab1e5eea4dc4d7ab90f.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $7.$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+7 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,9\\}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=9,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100961 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "506f52f3-8093-5bee-a2f5-f30f064dc715" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "238fef3c-4f22-5034-924b-0380723ee9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510857} {:answer/id #uuid "295da837-a754-53d4-ac11-249c126eceb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510867} {:answer/id #uuid "36f1c22b-e984-57a9-89c4-5c0b33dfae7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510905} {:answer/id #uuid "bd46d8fd-7d57-5880-a3f6-77bb4a90c0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511171} {:answer/id #uuid "c6e926