heorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =3 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3-6 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3,3\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511489 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5bcc62c-f716-503f-9195-b1f8f1c78e04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b09df16-6636-5d5a-a07e-b977e5c99a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510801} {:answer/id #uuid "2ead93a4-4662-5088-8f13-942128d4fcae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510883} {:answer/id #uuid "4291ba92-5f2c-5dc3-a5db-2d7ca1b77cfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510931} {:answer/id #uuid "5670001a-de2c-5e94-bfc2-8dd60d548162" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510966} {:answer/id #uuid "809430b8-a1ca-5594-b25f-9c41dda2a948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b09df16-6636-5d5a-a07e-b977e5c99a48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510801} :answer-field/id #uuid "878756a2-046d-51e8-8047-b9ae0e732f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "488dbbc3-e305-587b-8387-bc6f8dfc6d19" :question/math-academy-id "q-15742" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8500caba027f22623c3e20287f92f12e56aad667788708189f68205f4122195.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,9].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4,$ $8,$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,9]$ given that $f(0)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,9]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,9),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3,7.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,7,9.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =1 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(7) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{7}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(9) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{9}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+4-8+2 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{1,5,-3,-1\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d05ee4-a3f9-5845-aabf-04165b4653e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510787} {:answer/id #uuid "11fc7f6f-2388-5e11-b63d-7af13c584f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510816} {:answer/id #uuid "3766ec0d-0113-5d0c-ad91-d63c83936a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510909} {:answer/id #uuid "619f3b03-eef6-5b71-ac90-870978677b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510989} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f3b9f0-1089-5653-bb60-63efb1012505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "619f3b03-eef6-5b71-ac90-870978677b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510989} :answer-field/id #uuid "eafdb283-9289-5258-96cf-a740ad61cf63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "907c4d01-952e-54f5-819a-03c35c5b1210" :question/math-academy-id "q-15744" :question/problem "![](images/39/392562d7675b8ebddd33cbdea448481654f9e643b088b949d4337618517c537b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[0,5]$ given that $f(0)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,1,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/82/82f6ace89a1d880390b5f6c2dbe344a6230d5bc91ba11521ac996b5e59ebc49c.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(1) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{4,\\frac{11}{2},4,5\\}=\\frac{11}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{11}{2},$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5bcc62c-f716-503f-9195-b1f8f1c78e04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d9063ef-6952-5b4a-b585-5b15a5a0b4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099026} {:answer/id #uuid "29beae2b-e3ad-54c1-b38c-77047fe5ae6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099027} {:answer/id #uuid "3f07a31e-afd5-574c-b84a-4c775953dcd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099028} {:answer/id #uuid "7c282323-a32d-5969-83ed-d938a4ad4bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099029} {:answer/id #uuid "7f9ab6ce-37b7-526f-92ce-0279e52b7b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d9063ef-6952-5b4a-b585-5b15a5a0b4c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099026} :answer-field/id #uuid "468f70d4-e1fe-51a6-a43a-7fc0681f374d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186099025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da3b0feb-e320-535c-8435-9a7c6b6e71cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-18650" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f2d06871f14bead2e13d9fcb373560dcd74b30f74c3d8f9a6319762130ec162.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-3,3]$ given that $f(-3)=-6.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-6 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4 \\\\  & =-10 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6-4+2 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-6,-10,-8\\}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b167983-81a9-54ba-b03a-a6f83001fc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b167983-81a9-54ba-b03a-a6f83001fc2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510872} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d3a2c9-a5db-5dc5-82dc-e70759c371f4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de089661-beea-5ee0-bfa5-18ccd2aea3c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-316990" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4077eba1972d32e7ab9b7bfbe550654395412076f175f48b1d78a49a21641ae.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[0,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ if $f(0)=5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of two trapezoids. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/aa/aade60ca0b004cad7f0bb1e2de4e5ea354e1f87625b3d915e745381c489c41a4.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5 \\\\  & =7.5 \\\\ f(6) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5+2.5-4 \\\\  & =3.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{5,7.5,3.5\\}=7.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=7.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100961 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "506f52f3-8093-5bee-a2f5-f30f064dc715" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc09cc98-aa3a-5fb7-8896-022fdc71a474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc09cc98-aa3a-5fb7-8896-022fdc71a474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511302} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a1b22c-006e-5530-8085-81d9408aea6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "47b1ac6a-9da0-5717-9e95-4b0413ae2c6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343665" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7aa23647a61b4d13e9468eafc34947e8b01046a31d67da8ee997476e13dec95.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $2\\pi .$ Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-3\\pi .$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-3\\pi  \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\pi -2\\pi  \\\\  & =-5\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-3\\pi ,-5\\pi \\}=-3\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=-3\\pi ,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5bcc62c-f716-503f-9195-b1f8f1c78e04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f93c0d67-7bd9-5f1c-85c2-95df089580db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f93c0d67-7bd9-5f1c-85c2-95df089580db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511292} :answer-field/id #uuid "b71666a0-c57b-5c11-820a-d694a30bc7a9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511412}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "065ae9b3-7009-5705-869a-1bfc3920deab" :question/math-academy-id "q-343672" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5f0d970e95b7e2e2656ef579f9d5be49eb9eddb433a5921149347ef349139c5.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-3,5].$ The graph of ${g}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $g(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4$ and $6$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $g(x)$ on the interval $[-3,5]$ given that $g(-3)=2.$\n\n${g}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $g(x)$ on $[-3,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${g}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,5),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,0,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $g$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =2 \\\\ g(0) & =g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{0}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ g(5) & =g(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{5}{g}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(4-6) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $g$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $g$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{g}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,6,0\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $g$ is ${g}_{\\text{max}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $g(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5bcc62c-f716-503f-9195-b1f8f1c78e04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97ad1452-16bf-5aaa-b266-a0c4eefba9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97ad1452-16bf-5aaa-b266-a0c4eefba9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511096} :answer-field/id #uuid "8811494e-64ac-563f-b9e3-157298f88e64" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b3942d1-426d-544a-a802-5cef91893443" :question/math-academy-id "q-343680" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4b4735b3154603284ed6f232ae640c5f47a536a5c26b75bb99970c8d0cb2c08.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,2].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $h(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $3$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $h(x)$ on the interval $[-4,2]$ given that $h(-4)=-6.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $h(x)$ on $[-4,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,2),$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-4) & =-6 \\\\ h(-1) & =h(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6+5 \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ h(2) & =h(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{2}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6+(5-3) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $h$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-6,-1,-4\\}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{max}}=-1,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5bcc62c-f716-503f-9195-b1f8f1c78e04" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value Given a Derivative Graph With More Than One Area"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80f28cbc-9bc5-54e6-97e1-466a4f97e761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80f28cbc-9bc5-54e6-97e1-466a4f97e761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511046} :answer-field/id #uuid "4003c727-78b9-502a-bc48-15371f44dc21" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "27335de7-ab94-5f77-a70c-95b3aeec92aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-343704" :question/problem "![](images/a3/a3f57d0658f89f37df9a838b4a79cb9d4ed3d4183187013923c26fb3bd836e10.png)\n\nThe function $k(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,7].$ The graph of ${k}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $k(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $4,$ $7,$ and $2$ respectively (from left to right). Find the maximum value of $k(x)$ on the interval $[-2,7]$ given that $k(-2)=2.$\n\n${k}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $k(x)$ on $[-2,7]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${k}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,7),$ and ${k}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1,5.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,1,5,7.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $k$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k(-2) & =2 \\\\ k(1) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{1}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2-4 \\\\  & =-2 \\\\ k(5) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{5}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-4+7) \\\\  & =5 \\\\ k(7) & =k(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{7}{k}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2+(-4+7-2) \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $k$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $k$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{k}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{2,-2,5,3\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $k$ is ${k}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $k(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "759a68b1-2538-543b-9c09-1d4d9da7d95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "759a68b1-2538-543b-9c09-1d4d9da7d95c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511025} :answer-field/id #uuid "262dabb7-a19e-557c-b02a-f2846842eb57" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "457b4315-a413-5f6e-81e5-9a7aa0226cf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-343884" :question/problem "![](images/01/01dad591c5f02e0528e9092a058b44806cb0bab6f709347f8f90120c58ad25bc.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-3,4].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ if $f(-3)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-3,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-3,4),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-3,2,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function  ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/eb/ebcaf43d56e8854ce17d89ce66a811ad1f505f7ebb855362798d845164a80377.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =-2 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+7.5 \\\\  & =5.5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(-3)+{\\int }_{-3}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(7.5-1) \\\\  & =4.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-2,5.5,4.5\\}=5.5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5.5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e051c3b2-7e07-5470-85d6-e4ed12752211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e051c3b2-7e07-5470-85d6-e4ed12752211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511247} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef945b41-3caa-5828-922f-822c233714fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c4376358-d419-5ca5-9d5f-60ee48a180a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343902" :question/problem "![](images/86/8678ae0607275abf4732943add33116314569efc3afb44e92a15d6e78eeacd64.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ if $f(-4)=-5.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,1,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of one square, two trapezoids, and one triangle. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/7f/7fe0ee64fd29812d2ee536d7285f5e4674d8985a2dc1e27bf230dafb0159046e.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-5 \\\\ f(1) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5+(1+5+4) \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5-3 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{-5,5,2\\}=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=5,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96c23e89-1183-51f8-96e1-860b01688bd1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Maximum Value by Computing Areas Under Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149ea365-6c9e-5896-b572-37eac2a934cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "149ea365-6c9e-5896-b572-37eac2a934cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510825} :answer-field/id #uuid "28d8d771-54ee-598d-ba3a-511af4adfe5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511332}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a52ab505-f7f2-54ed-afb3-b43bed5cfef5" :question/math-academy-id "q-343959" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cb97b20d164c2976ae046f83edbbb499514ff3c7ddfbfa9b703918457eba467.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,6].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find the maximum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,6]$ given that $f(-4)=7.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{max}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidate test, the maximum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,6]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,6),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2$ and $x=3.$  Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3,6.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of  $f(x)$  for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function ${f}^{′}(x)$ is made up of $3$ triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/e6/e67e51128fe4392e814eec6d9d6ab17d7d7718af38fd556a0a47c603174e9dcd.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =7, \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =7+2 \\\\  & =9, \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =9+15 \\\\  & =24, \\\\ f(6) & =f(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{6}{f}^{′}(x)\\,dx \\\\  & =24-4.5 \\\\  & =19.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the largest value of $f$ from our candidates. \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{max}}=max\\{7,9,24,19.5\\}=24.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the maximum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{max}}=24,$ and the value of $x$ that maximizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{max}}=3.$"}]}
